MHG. ''zeln'', G. ''zählen'', number (''erzählen'', narrate), = Icel. ''telja'' = Sw. ''tälja'' = Dan. ''tælle'', number, tell; cf. Goth. ''talzjan'', instruct, direct; from the noun represented by ''tale''<sup>1</sup>: see ''tale''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. ''tale''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'' For the forms ''tell'', ''told'', cf. ''sell'', ''sold''.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To number; count; enumerate; reckon one by one, or one after another: as, to ''tell'' a hundred; to ''tell'' one's beads.

{{smaller block|Certeyn I hem never ''tolde'';
For as fele eyen hadde she
As fetheres upon foules be.
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 1380.}}}}

{{smaller block|His custom was to ''tell'' over his herd of sea-calves at noon, and then to sleep. ''Bacon'', Physical Fables, vii.}}

{{smaller block|He cannot be so innocent a coxcomb;
He can ''tell'' ten, sure.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Coxcomb, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Nobody comes to visit him, he receives no letters, and ''tells'' his money morning and evening.
{{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 264.}}}}

2. To recount; rehearse; narrate; relate: as, to ''tell'' a story.

{{smaller block|Witnesse, ye Heavens, the truth of all that I have ''teld''!
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VII. vi. 27.}}}}

{{smaller block|Life . . . is a tale
''Told'' by an idiot, full of sound and fury,
Signifying nothing. ''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 5. 27.}}

{{smaller block|Masters, I have to ''tell'' a tale of woe,
A tale of folly and of wasted life.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 5.}}}}

3. To make known; divulge; disclose; reveal; communicate: as, to ''tell'' a secret; to ''tell'' one's errand.

{{smaller block|Now wul y ''telle'' the rygt Way to Jerusalem.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 125.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|''Tell'' it not in Gath, publish it not in the streets of Askelon.
{{right|2 Sam. i. 20.}}}}

{{smaller block|She never ''told'' her love,
But let concealment, like a worm i' the bud,
Feed on her damask cheek.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., ii. 4. 113.}}}}

{{smaller block|I wonder wha 's ''tauld'' that gay ladie
The fashion into our countrie.
{{right|''Lord Dingwall'' (Child's Ballads, I. 290).}}}}

4. To declare; say.

{{smaller block|Who-so contrarieth treuthe he ''telleth'' in the gospel
That God knoweth hym nouȝte, ne no seynte of heuene.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), v. 55.}}}}

5. To put or express in words; recite; explain; make clear or plain.

{{smaller block|And dede men for that deon [din] comen oute of deope graues,
And ''tolden'' why that tempest so longe tyme durede.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xxi. 66.}}}}

{{smaller block|I know, quoth he, what it meaneth, but I cannot ''tell'' it; I cannot express it.
{{right|''Latimer'', 2d Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1550.}}}}

{{smaller block|Whoso ask'd her for his wife,
His riddle ''told'' not, lost his life.
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, i., Prol., l. 38.}}}}

{{smaller block|Few can ''tell'' his pedigree,
Nor his subtill nature coster.
{{right|''Marston and Barksted'', Insatiate Countess, v.}}}}

6. To discern so as to be able to say; distinguish; recognize; decide; determine: as, to ''tell'' one from another; she cannot ''tell'' which she likes best.

{{smaller block|I could always ''tell'' if visitors had called in my absence.
{{right|''Thoreau'', Walden, p. 141.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
7. To inform.

{{smaller block|He seith that ye be sone aperceyvaunte of hym, and that ye sholde ''telle'' me what he is.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 74.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Tell'' me, good Hobbinoll, what garres thee greete?
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., April.}}}}

{{smaller block|I'll ''tell'' you as we pass along,
That you will wonder what hath fortuned.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., v. 4. 168.}}}}

8. To give an order, command, or direction to; order; bid: as, I ''told'' him to stay at home.

{{smaller block|Call for your casting-bottle, and place your mirror in your hat, as I ''told'' you. ''B. Jonson'', Cynthia's Revels, ii. 1.}}

{{smaller block|It may be accepted as necessary for the comfort of all coachmen that a team should never start until ''told''.
{{right|''New York Tribune'', May 11, 1890.}}}}

9. To assure; assert positively to.

{{smaller block|They are burs, I can ''tell'' you. ''Shak.'', T. and C., iii. 2. 120.}}

{{smaller block|Pshaw! I ''tell'' you 'tis no such thing—you are the man she wants, and nobody but you.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Duenna, ii. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|Let me ''tell'' you, you may drink worse French wine in many taverns in London than they have sometimes at this house. ''Cotton'', in Walton's Angler, ii. 227.}}

10†. To make account of: in phrases such as ''to tell no tale, to tell no dainty, to tell no store.''

{{smaller block|Vesselle of Sylver is there non: for thei ''telle'' no prys there of, to make no Vesselle offe.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 220.}}}}

{{smaller block|I ne ''tolde no deyntee'' of hir love.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Wife of Bath's Tale, l. 208.}}}}

'''Tell that to the marines.''' See ''marine''.—'''To tell noses.''' See ''nose''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To tell no store of†.''' See ''store''<sup>3</sup>.—'''To tell off,''' to count off; especially, to count off and detach, as for some special duty: as, a squad was ''told off'' to clear the streets.—'''To tell one's beads.''' See ''to bid beads'',

