'''II.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To give an account; make report; speak; explain: with ''of.''

{{smaller block|Bothe of yonge and olde<br>Ful wel byloved, and wel folk ''of'' hire ''tolde.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 131.}}}}

{{smaller block|That I may publish with the voice of thanksgiving, and ''tell of'' all thy wondrous works. {{right|Ps. xxvi. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|This ancient and isolated city [Ragusa] has yet something more to ''tell of.'' {{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 240.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Of'' the fruitful year<br>They ''told,'' and its delights.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 392.}}}}

2. To say; declare.

{{smaller block|For hit aren murye-mouthede men mynstrales of heuene,<br>And godes boyes, bordiours as the bok ''telleth.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), x. 127.}}}}

3. To talk; chat; gossip. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|While I've been ''telling'' with you, here 've this little maid been and ate up all my sugar!
{{right|''Kingsley'', Westward Ho, xxx.}}}}

4. To tell tales; play the informer; inform; blab: with ''of'' or ''on'' before the person: as, if you do, I'll ''tell.'' [Now colloq.]

{{smaller block|And David saved neither man nor woman alive, to bring tidings to Gath, saying, Lest they should ''tell on'' us, saying, So did David. {{right|1 Sam. xxvii. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|He didn't want to ''tell on'' Maggie, though he was angry with her; for Tom Tulliver was a lad of honor.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, i. 5.}}}}

5. To act effectively; produce a marked effect or impression; count for something.

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|It's true, every year will ''tell'' upon him. He is over five-and-forty, you know.|{{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, iv.}}}}

{{smaller block|It would seem that even pedantry and antiquarianism are welcomed when they ''tell'' on behalf of the other side.|{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 42.}}}}

{{smaller block|Everybody knows that speeches are little, that debates are often nothing, in Congress and elsewhere; but votes ''tell''. It is the vote that men want.|{{right|''Bibliotheca Sacra'', XLVII. 544.}}}}

'''To hear tell of.''' {{smaller|See ''hear''.}}

'''tell'''<sup>1</sup> (tel), ''n.'' [< ''tell''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] That which is told; account; narration; story; tale. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|There, I am at the end of my ''tell!'' If I write on, it must be to ask questions.|{{right|''Walpole'', To Mann, April 4, 1743.}}}}

{{smaller block|Little Barb'ry 's the very flower of the flock, accordin' to my ''tell''.|{{right|''E. Eggleston'', The Century, XXXV. 44.}}}}

'''tell'''<sup>2</sup> (tel), ''n.'' [< Ar. ''tell'', a hill.] A hill or mound: common in Oriental place-names.

{{smaller block|The east bank of the Tigris, where gigantic ''tells'' or artificial mounds, and the traces of an ancient city wall, bore evident witness of fallen greatness.|{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XVII. 511.}}}}

'''tellable''' (tel′a-bl), ''a.'' [< ''tell''<sup>1</sup> + ''-able.''] Capable of being told; worth telling.

'''tell-bill-willy''' (tel′bil-wil″i), ''n.'' [Imitative.] The willet, ''Symphemia semipalmata.'' See cuts under ''willet'' and ''semipalmate.'' [Bahamas.]

'''tell-clock†''' (tel′klok), ''n.'' [< ''tell''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + obj. ''clock''<sup>2</sup>.] One who sits and counts the hours; an idler.

